page 3.\n\n**Theorem 2.3.1 (provided in the source).**\n\n**(a)** If $f$ is continuous on an open rectangle\n\n$$\nR:\\{a<x<b,\\ c<y<d\\}\n$$\n\nthat contains $(x_0,y_0)$, then the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'=f(x,y),\\qquad y(x_0)=y_0 \\tag{2.3.1}\n$$\n\nhas at least one solution on some open subinterval of $(a,b)$ that contains $x_0$.\n\n**(b)** If both $f$ and $f_y$ are continuous on $R$, then (2.3.1) has a unique solution on some open subinterval of $(a,b)$ that contains $x_0$.\n\nConsider the initial value problems of the form\n\n$$\ny'=(x+y)^{1/3},\\qquad y(x_0)=y_0.\n$$\n\nHint: The function $h(s)=s^{1/3}$ is continuous for all $s$.\n\nYou do not need to identify all rectangles. However, to receive full credit, you must clearly identify $f(x,y)$ and $f_y(x,y)$ and state where each is continuous." :multistep/id #uuid "dba7212a-4d89-5a5f-a7bc-edab78a3db60" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670611}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672070 :multistep/context "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(e), PDF page 6.\n\n**Conservation of Energy.** In physics, use the potential convention $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$, the negative of the usual mathematical convention. An object of constant mass $m$ moves in a conservative net force field $\\mathbf F$ along $\\mathbf r(t)$; write $\\mathbf v=\\mathbf r\\prime$. The source notes that Earth’s radial gravity field has exponent $p=3$ and is conservative by Problem 3. Follow the requested derivation step by step." :multistep/id #uuid "0844cc55-8a4e-5fc7-af0d-f8a4b0d28441" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672196}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672351}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672232}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672092 :multistep/context "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(c), PDF page 6.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle y,x\\rangle$, $O=(0,0)$, $A=(x,0)$, and $B=(x,y)$. Set $\\phi(O)=0$. Find a potential using the two specified line segments, rather than replacing the required method with direct partial integration." :multistep/id #uuid "199b980f-8f64-5c31-94ae-c9a300f2cfad" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672162}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672403}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672108 :multistep/context "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 12](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_2/12).\n\nThe equation $y+4y^7=(y^5+6x)y\\prime$ can be written as $M(x,y)dx+N(x,y)dy=0$." :multistep/id #uuid "2248e0aa-86e1-5889-9d81-eb302d401692" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672394}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672159 :multistep/context "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 3](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_2/3).\n\nSolve $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x}{36y}$." :multistep/id #uuid "440bd214-ce06-58ce-91d5-600494fbe863" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672203}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672252 :multistep/context "Source question: Problem 1(a–b), question PDF page 1; supplied key page 1.\n\nA direction field with labeled curves $A$–$F$ is shown below. No supporting work is required for this problem.\n\n![Direction field and labeled curves A through F](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FSG-1%2Fdirection-field-curves.png)" :multistep/id #uuid "824d4265-7a2f-5b54-a91c-69fb7a994285" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672164}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672248}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672329 :multistep/context "Source question: Problem 2(a)–(c), PDF page 3.\n\nAn object moves in the given force field from the initial point to the terminal point. Either find the work done or explain why there is not enough data." :multistep/id #uuid "bb6c85d8-e0b1-52d0-9c93-93ef070eabd0" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672180}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672350}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672331 :multistep/context "Source question: Problem 1(a)–(d), PDF page 2.\n\nFor each statement, explain why it is true or why it is false." :multistep/id #uuid "bc2a780d-42ab-5f12-821e-0802508f2414" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672286}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672342}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672222}}]}]}
